::JOUR_U04_L05_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.MED.05] Source: Title: The Quiet Work Before Opening Night. Intro: The auditorium is dim except for the blue glow of the stage lights. A ladder leans beside the curtain, scripts rest open on the front row, and a student sweeps sawdust from the edge of the set. Caption 1: Behind the bright posters and applause, the stage crew builds a small world from tape, paint, and patient hands. Caption 2: The empty seats wait in silence while the cast repeats the final scene for the fifth time. Caption 3: By Friday night, the nervous quiet will become music, movement, and a room full of voices. Design note: The package uses low-light photographs, close-up images of hands and tools, and short captions to create a reflective mood about preparation. Question 1: Which mood is best supported by the source? {
~A. The source creates a comic mood because the auditorium has seats.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the exact word, phrase, claim, or appeal named in the question. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misreads mood, ignores language effect, confuses rhetoric with topic, overstates the claim, or adds unsupported information.
~B. The mood is angry because students are working before the show.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the exact word, phrase, claim, or appeal named in the question. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misreads mood, ignores language effect, confuses rhetoric with topic, overstates the claim, or adds unsupported information.
=C. The source creates a reflective, expectant mood through silence, dim light, and preparation details.#Correct. Step 1: identify the language, mood, figurative language, rhetorical choice, claim, or appeal named in the question. Step 2: connect that choice to its effect on the audience using the embedded source. This answer works because it explains function and effect instead of only naming the topic.
~D. The mood is random because several objects are mentioned.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and locate the exact word, phrase, claim, or appeal named in the question. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misreads mood, ignores language effect, confuses rhetoric with topic, overstates the claim, or adds unsupported information.
}
